y each applicant that receives  an interview offer  is going to be matched finally i should  mention that in this  case we don't really have the interview  process  but we're still calling  the acceptance of applications an  interview offer just to be consistent  throughout the talk  now let's solve the easy case where both  the limit on the number of applications  and on the number of interviews is one  each applicant is applying to one  position  so in total we're going to have n  applications and remember from our model  that  all the preferences are independent so  we have n  independent applications sent uniformly  and independently to the positions  so it's easy to find the probability  that the position receives an  application it's actually a simple  wall symptoms analysis and when  n goes to infinity this approaches 1  minus 1 over e  in this case since each applicant has  only one application  they are going to receive at most one  interview offer  so they're going to accept it anyway and  the number of  positions that received an application  is going to be  the same as the number of applicants  that are matched  okay now let's get back to the  more general case of arbitrary number of  applications  in the single interview offer  it took us some time to realize that  this also has a very simple  solution and it's going to fit on one  slide  our first insight is that when n  approaches infinity we may assume that  the applications are  independent so technically with a finite  n different applications of a single  applicant are not independent they are  going to k distinct positions but as  n goes to infinity even if we draw a  fresh sample from the independent  from the uniform distribution the  probability that  this is going to be the same position as  the k minus one other application tends  to zero  so in the limit we may assume that all  applications of a single applicant are  independent  previously we knew that the applications  of different applicants  are also independent so basically we  have  n times k applications that are sent  independently and uniformly at random to  the positions  as a side note this is kind of like a  new variation of the balls in balance  problem  so the balls here are the applicants  and each we can think that there are  multiple copies of each ball  and these are all sent independently and  uniformly  to the bins and the bins have capacity  of one  and for example the problem that you're  studying here is the number of  distinct balls that fit into the bins  okay back to our problem so with  n times k and applications sent  independently to the positions  we can find the probability of an  application being accepted  so by definition it's going to be the  total number of accepted applications  over the total number of applications  okay  and there is a one-to-one correspondence  between the accepted applications  and the positions that have received any  application because each position that  receives  any application is going to exactly  accept one application  all right and  we can find the number or the expected  number of positions with applications  pretty easily similar to our analysis on  the last slide  so with nk applications this is going to  be the probability that  um a position receives any application  so this times  n is the number of positions with  applications  and the total number of applications is  n times k  all right so now from  a random applicant's point of view each  of their applications  have the same probability p of being  accepted  therefore the probability that an  applicant  receives any interview offer is going to  be  1 minus 1 minus p to the k and we are  substituting  p from here so we get this number and  from two slides ago we know that  the size of the matching in this case is  the number of applicants that  have any accepted applications  all right so let's see  what is what this function looks like as  a function of k  all right okay so 