 applies to x positions  and another applicant applies to y  positions  while the difference between these  numbers is at least two  we can redistribute these applications  and  make their number of applications closer  to each other and increase the size of  the matching  and the proof is actually  straightforward all right  so it has a simple corollary  that says that given any average number  of applications  x the size of the matching is maximized  if the applicants either apply to x  number of positions or ceiling x number  of positions  basically applying to the same number of  positions or two consecutive numbers  is the best way of distributing the  applications  okay so let's see what is the size of  the matching in this case  all right so we have x as the average  number of applications  and we're going to have this fraction of  people applying to  florex applications and this fraction  applying to  ceiling x application and the  probability of  the applicants being matched is similar  to the  solution from the previous slides  and we can also find a  p similar to before all right  now let's see the shape of this figure  okay so again on the x-axis we have the  average number of applications and the  y-axis  is the size of the matching or fraction  of matched agents  and for example at 2.5 half of the  applicants are applying to two positions  while the second half are applying to  three positions okay  so here we see our second interesting  fact  the u-shaped between the two integers  so what is basically happening  is that if you start from so first of  all  it means that having all the applicants  applied to three positions is the best  even if you're allowed to pick other  distributions of applications  so three applications is still optimal  and  when we have a limited number of  interviews we need to also limit the  number of applications  to have a good size of matching and  secondly  the maximum magic is achieved with a  fair system of  allocating 